  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 338 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nPost-cold frontal, continental air mass continues to spread\nthroughout our region behind yesterday's cold front/system.\nBreezy winds will taper tonight as surface high pressure settles\ninto the region, centered near northwest MN by Wednesday\nmorning. This will promote calm and clear conditions, allowing\nfrost to develop within northwest MN, in addition to the \npotential for fog in northwest and west-central MN. Confidence\nis not high in fog formation, but there is a 40% chance it\ndevelops within our area. If it does develop, fog could be dense\nat times.\n\nUpper pattern mid to late week can be characterized as zonal \nto weak southwesterly flow aloft. This will offer opportunities\nfor periods of rain starting Wednesday, lasting into the\nweekend. This will also keep temperatures trended near to\nslightly above average.\n\n...Frost potential in northwest MN...\n\nCenter of high pressure settles into northwest MN tonight/early\nWed amid relatively dry continental air mass. This will \nradiatively cool temperatures into the mid to upper 30s, \nperhaps locally lower 30s if conditions remain optimal.\n\nWhile mid/upper cloud cover is not anticipated to be a failure-\nmode for frost development, the possibility of fog in this area\nis. General thinking is for areas that received more than half \nan inch of recent rainfall (generally south of the Red Lakes) \nwill promote more of a chance for fog development compared to \nareas north. With this in mind, confidence has grown enough in \nfrost development in northwest MN near Lake of the Woods, and \nnot including Bemidji/southern Beltrami County.\n\n...Rain chances ahead...\n\nZonal flow aloft with entrance region of an upper jet will \nlinger over our region through Wednesday, allowing a weak and \nprogressive shortwave trough/upper ascent to migrate west to \neast across our area. While synoptic forcing is expected to be\nweak and moisture return meager, an uptick in instability is\nforecast to accompany this forcing/moisture. This will offer\nopportunity for thunderstorms of the weaker variety. Very little\nrainfall amounts are expected with this, despite the convective\nbehavior.\n\nOn Friday, a rather robust banded-rain event is expected with\nstrong forcing from frontogenesis fed by rich moisture and\ninstability. Current guidance keeps majority of this rainfall\n(in excess of 3 inches) south of our area out of eastern SD into\nsouthern/central MN. However, given the majority of this forcing\ndriven by mesoscale processes, confidence in location is not\ngreat. Thus, there is potential for this banded heavy rainfall\nto eek into locations near the SD border into west-central MN.\n\nInto this weekend, a more consolidated shortwave trough is\nprogged to translate across the Dakotas into Minnesota west to\neast. This will bring widespread rainfall chances. However,\nconfidence is low in evolution of this particular wave given its\ntime into the future nearing the predictability horizon. At \nthis time, there is very little evidence to suggest amounts more\nthan 1 inch from this particular wave.",
